WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.410
Spider-Man movies have come a long way since
the Sam Raimi days.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.380
But maybe you still have a few questions.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.120
Like, what is BARF?

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.760
Why was Tony Stark such a bad boss?

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.940
And is Mysterio really dead?

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.520
We'll get into all this and more as we break
down the ending of Spider-Man: Far From Home.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:22.940
Over the course of five movies, Iron Man has
been Peter's mentor and personal role model,

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.600
taking over what's traditionally been the
Uncle Ben role.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.540
As a result, this is another non-Iron Man
MCU movie that sure has a lot of Tony Stark.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:32.580
"...you miss me?"

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:37.420
This time around, Tony's hogging of the spotlight
is even more impressive; since being fried

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:41.500
by the full might of the Infinity Stones,
that guy is extremely dead.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:45.940
And the world loves him for it, with murals
on walls honoring the hero from the streets

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.780
of Queens to the canals of Venice.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.839
While Tony's left a gigantic legacy behind
for Peter to draw from, Peter is having to

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:54.800
learn to get by on his own, sort of.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.120
Through the use of over-the-top technology,
Stark is still watching over him.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:03.920
Instead of building a suit of armor around
the world a la Age of Ultron, Tony left gear

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.580
behind for Peter, a young man who clearly
reminded him of himself.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:13.220
But where Tony didn't realize his capacity
for heroism until well into adulthood, Peter

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:16.480
is putting his life on the line before he's
even gone through the trouble of learning

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:17.240
how to drive.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.470
It's an earnest desire to help that the movie's
villain, Jake Gyllenhaal's Mysterio, quickly

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:23.980
learns to use against him.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:29.540
In another instance of the movie leaning on
the established MCU, Far From Home features

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:34.700
the return of Tony Stark's Binarily Augmented
Retro-Framing technology, or BARF.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:36.860
"God, I gotta work on that acronym."

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.160
BARF is an absurdly-advanced virtual reality
platform that we've previously seen in Captain

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:43.200
America: Civil War.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:47.740
In its previous appearance, this tech was
mostly used to show off Tony's home movies,

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:51.510
much to the frustration of its inventor, Jake
Gyllenhaal's Quentin Beck.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:56.060
BARF isn't the only piece of Stark tech that
Beck is using to put one over on the world.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:00.600
He's also after the surprisingly-important
pair of glasses Tony was previously seen wearing

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:05.210
casually during the events of Infinity War,
and that he bequeathed to Peter Parker following

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:05.840
his death.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.400
"Either put on these glasses, or start eating
that trash can."

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:13.100
Tony being Tony, these are no ordinary glasses.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:17.160
Beck's primary reason for wanting the glasses
has to do with their connection to a powerful

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.920
fleet of weaponized drones, capable of projecting
a massive illusion in London.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:27.299
The glasses are also seemingly capable of
tapping into every device connected to a computer

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.840
more-or-less instantaneously, a kind of terrifying
power.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.840
Is this what Tony was doing with those glasses
this whole time?

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.380
Spying on people's phones?

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:37.120
"I know that it's confusing…"

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:42.980
Tony being Tony, the EDITH name is an acronym
for "Even in Death, I'm the Hero."

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.480
But by the time EDITH is being used to help
Beck attack London with a combined elemental

00:02:47.489 --> 00:02:52.519
illusion, it feels like a more fitting name
would be "even in death, I think I know best,

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.620
and accidentally put millions of people at
risk."

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:57.360
No wonder so many of Tony's old employees
hate him.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.920
After manipulating Peter into handing over
EDITH, Mysterio celebrates with his surprisingly

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.220
robust staff.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:10.719
Hilariously, this squad of disgruntled middle-management
types is united by one thing: a lousy working

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.720
relationship with Anthony Edward Stark.

00:03:13.129 --> 00:03:17.340
One character, William Riva, is revealed to
have turned to the dark side under Mysterio

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:21.380
because Jeff Bridges yelled at him during
a tense moment in 2008's Iron Man.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.260
"Tony Stark was able to build this in a cave!"

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:28.960
He stayed mad about that encounter for more
than ten years.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:34.600
While helping out Mysterio during his staged
encounter with the molten fire elemental,

00:03:34.609 --> 00:03:38.719
Peter notices a little bit of strangeness
regarding the physics of the fight, with his

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.600
webs bouncing off what appear to be some invisible
objects near the under-attack Ferris wheel.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:47.140
Peter sort of shrugs off the strangeness,
at least initially.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.120
What really sells Peter on Mysterio's villainy
is MJ's recovery of a projector drone in the

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:56.999
aftermath of the battle, which displays pre-rendered
footage of one of the elementals in action.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.959
Seeing this footage, Peter realizes that the
elementals aren't real, and that Mysterio

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:03.080
is not to be trusted.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.920
After being beaten by Mysterio in his first
confrontation, Peter returns for a second

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:13.360
round, after designing himself a new suit
in very Iron Mannish fashion.

00:04:14.069 --> 00:04:18.780
Peter's goal during the final London encounter
is to take out the many drones Beck's deployed

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:23.690
to make his grand, Avengers-level illusion,
shutting him down before he can hurt any innocent

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:26.040
civilians, or kill Peter and his friends.

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:31.340
Peter smashes up the Stark drones in a variety
of ways, one of the more exciting of which

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:36.280
is his stylish deployment of electrically-charged
taser webbing to cause them to explode.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:40.820
While this tech might seem like a new addition
to Peter's upgraded suit, they're actually

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:46.080
a holdover from the first suit that Tony Stark
designed, the one that sported way more ways

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.840
to kill people than Peter would realistically
need.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:55.160
All in all, this final act shows Peter learning
to trust his gut, especially his "Peter tingle,"

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.040
which comes into play in his final showdown
with Mysterio.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:02.560
It also shows him fully embracing the trust
that Tony had placed in him.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:07.300
After realizing that Tony Stark made plenty
of mistakes throughout his Iron Man career,

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:11.500
Spider-Man stops beating himself up for giving
Beck control of EDITH and goes to work on

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:12.500
saving the day.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:16.960
And if diving headfirst into danger to fix
horrible, world-threatening mistakes you've

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:20.940
made isn't living up to the legacy of Tony
Stark, what is?

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:23.140
"Not bad."

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:28.480
Mysterio meets his end thanks to Spider-Man's
sheer persistence, with the hero fighting

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.860
while physically outnumbered and under psychological
attack.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:35.760
By midway through the battle, it's obvious
that Peter is going to win.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:40.660
Instead of despairing or running away, Mysterio
keeps his cool, and begins to make reference

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:42.940
to an enigmatic contingency plan.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:48.440
Peter makes his way closer to Beck, wrecking
his drones and using his Spider-Sense to blast

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:51.120
through the illusions that BARF tech throws
up.

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:55.820
He also defends himself against a hail of
bullets that Mysterio fires at him, one of

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:58.680
which strikes Mysterio himself, apparently
fatally.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:03.620
But of course, considering the scale of Beck's
illusions, it's impossible not to wonder if

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:04.960
he's actually a goner.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:09.080
There's plenty of evidence from the comics​
to make this a legitimate question.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:13.960
Not only has Quentin Beck faked his own death
before, but several other criminals have taken

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:16.260
on the identity of Mysterio in Beck's place.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:21.260
In short, there's plenty of room for the Master
of Illusion to make a return one way or another.

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:25.980
And either way, unbeknownst to Peter, Mysterio's
backup plan is now in motion.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:31.020
After Spidey halts the attack, one of the
people on Mysterio's payroll can be seen downloading

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:35.440
data onto a portable drive before walking
away with unknown intentions.

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:39.680
It's implied that what's been copied is data
related to the fight, specifically camera

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:41.220
footage from the drones.

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:46.000
It ends up coming back in a big way during
the mid-credits scene, but for all anyone

00:06:46.009 --> 00:06:50.860
knows in the hours after the battle, Mysterio
has been defeated, and the threat he poses

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:51.440
is over.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:55.340
Unfortunately, poor Peter Parker is far from
done with this fight.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:59.520
Suffice to say, it looks like this Peter can't
even get through high school with his secret

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:00.560
identity intact.

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:05.639
If only he had gone into the battle dressed
as Night Monkey, this probably wouldn't have

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:06.900
been such a big deal.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:10.120
The comics once again offer some precedent
for these plot points.

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:15.680
Back in Mysterio's first appearance in ​Amazing
Spider-Man ​#13​, he poses as a crimefighter

00:07:15.689 --> 00:07:20.469
and strikes a deal with J. Jonah Jameson​
to expose Spidey's identity to the world in

00:07:20.469 --> 00:07:22.060
the pages of ​The Daily Bugle​.

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:26.220
What we see in the mid-credits scene is a
clear reference to Mysterio's debut.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.039
Before all of that goes down, Peter returns
to New York, having finally established a

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:32.980
romantic relationship with MJ.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:35.520
That's not the only relationship on Peter's
mind, though.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:40.420
He also has questions for Happy Hogan and
Aunt May regarding their relationship status

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.700
in the new, post-Blip world order.

00:07:43.069 --> 00:07:47.219
After interrogating his guardian about her
love life, Peter rolls his eyes and sets off

00:07:47.219 --> 00:07:51.509
for a triumphant session of swinging around
the buildings of Manhattan, a well-earned

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:52.140
victory lap.

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:57.280
It's a way different feel from Peter's first
adventures in Homecoming, which mostly stayed

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:01.100
across the East River in the slightly-less-vertical
borough of Queens.

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:05.660
More than any of Peter's other adventures
in the MCU so far, this sequence feels like

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:10.889
a throwback to the Sam Raimi Spider-Man movies,
which featured no shortage of exhilarating

00:08:10.889 --> 00:08:12.120
Manhattan-based web-slinging.

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:17.580
Charmingly, Peter doesn't go it alone, taking
MJ up into the skies to swing downtown for

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:18.400
a few blocks.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:23.060
Judging by her excitedly panicked reaction,
it's something she'll have to get used to,

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:26.100
but despite the danger, she still looks happy
to give it a whirl.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:29.060
If the movie ended here, it'd be a happy note.

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:34.380
Unfortunately for Peter, the movie's post-credit
scenes have a major change in store for him,

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:38.800
one that looks primed to permanently shake
up Spider-Man's place in the MCU.

